  "query_preview": "(Today through Monday)...\nIssued at 238 AM EST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nA rainy Thanksgiving is in store for central Indiana, with the \npotential for some snowflakes mixing in for parts of the area. Total \nrainfall amounts between 1 and 2 inches are possible.\n\nEarly This Morning through Tonight...\n\nIndy has finally had its first hard freeze early this morning with \nmostly clear skies. Readings across the remainder of central Indiana \nwere in the middle 20s to around 30. \n\nHigh clouds will be on the increase through tonight ahead of the \napproaching system. Readings in the 20s early in the morning will \nrebound into the lower to middle 40s this afternoon. High pressure \nwill keep very dry air in place, with dewpoints in the teens for \nsome areas this afternoon.\n\nClouds should have enough thin spots in them this evening to allow \ntemperatures to cool decently. Low temperatures in the mid to upper \n20s are expected, but as clouds thicken up overnight, readings \nshould become steady or even rebound a couple of degrees.\n\nWednesday...\n\nAn upper level low will move southeast into Missouri. This low will \npull moisture into the area from a southern stream upper low. \nForcing will increase during the day, especially west closer to the \nsystem. Precipitation will have to overcome the drier air at the \nsurface, but by late afternoon likely or higher PoPs will be across \nthe western forecast area with lower PoPs east. \n\nTemperatures will rise into the lower and middle 40s, making rain \nthe precipitation type.\n\nWednesday night through Friday...\n\nThe deepening upper low will move into Kentucky on Thanksgiving and \nthen move northeast into Friday. A cyclonically curved upper jet \nwill move into Tennessee and then east, providing additional \nforcing. A surface trough will eventually settle across the area on \nThanksgiving before moving northeast.\n\nPlentiful moisture will have worked into the area, and this will \ninteract with the forcing to bring widespread precipitation to \ncentral Indiana Wednesday night through Thursday night. \nPrecipitation chances will diminish from southwest to northeast on \nFriday as forcing exits.\n\nThe coldest air aloft should remain west and south of the area, \ncloser to the core of the upper system. Warm advection from the \nsoutheast and even east will help keep that colder air west of the \narea. At the surface, temperatures will generally be above freezing \ninto Friday. Thus, rain should be the primary precipitation type for \nthis event.\n\nThat being said, will have to keep an eye on the far western and \neven portions of the southern forecast area to see if some of that \ncolder air could sneak in and produce a little light snow at times, \nespecially at night when the near surface layer will be coolest.\n\nTotal rainfall amounts between 1 and 2 inches are possible. \nFrontogenetical forcing may create some locally heavier bands of \nrain. Since the area has been dry for a while now, flooding concerns \nare low.\n\nHighs on Thanksgiving and Friday will be in the lower and middle \n40s, with lows in the lower to middle 30s Wednesday night and \nThursday night.\n\nSaturday onward...\n\nNearly zonal flow will allow various upper systems to move through \nthe area during this period. Models differ on strength and timing of \nthese systems, lowering overall confidence in chances for rain for \nany one period.\n\nTemperatures will be near normal with highs in the upper 40s to \nlower 50s and lows in the 30s.",
